\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Structure of the LTN 1/20 evaluation framework, comprising the Cycling Level of Service (CLoS) and the Junction Assessment Tool (JAT), and their combined role in determining Active Travel England funding eligibility. Compiled by author from Department for Transport \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\textcite {departmentfortransport2020a}.}}{5}{figure.caption.9}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Study Area: The West Midlands. Left: location within England. Right: population distribution across the seven constituent metropolitan boroughs (Census 2021, 1 dot = 10 people), with hatched areas indicating rural Output Areas (OAs).}}{10}{figure.caption.11}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Analytical framework. LTN 1/20 design criteria (left) supply the classification thresholds operationalised on OS NGD Transport data (right) through Sections 4.1–4.3. The classified network is then evaluated against LTN 1/20's "Cycling for All" principle through connectivity analysis (Section 4.4) and network growth simulation (Section 4.5).}}{13}{figure.caption.13}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Directed cyclable network of the West Midlands Combined Authority by facility type. Where a road segment carries different provision by direction, the facility with the greater degree of separation from motor traffic is shown. The network is dominated by mixed traffic (grey) and off-road paths (purple). Physically separated infrastructure (blue) is concentrated along radial corridors into Birmingham and Coventry. }}{22}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Final Level of Traffic Stress classification across the directed cyclable network after junction stress enhancement. Each physical road link carries two directed edges (forward and backward) that may receive different junction-enhanced LTS scores where approach-level conflict differs by direction. Where the two directions disagree, the map displays the higher (more stressful) of the two values, as layers are drawn in ascending LTS order so that higher-stress edges overlay lower-stress ones on the shared link geometry. Basemap: OpenStreetMap contributors.}}{23}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Network-wide LTS composition by directed network length. Panel~A shows the overall distribution across all four LTS levels, with urban (solid fill) and rural (hatched fill) segments distinguished within each level. Panel~B provides context: the urban share of the study area by Census 2021 usual-resident population (99.0\%) and by land area (87.2\%). Panel~C decomposes the LTS profile separately for urban and rural segments, each normalised to 100\% of its own sub-network length. Rural areas show a markedly higher combined LTS~3 and~4 share (39.5\%) compared with urban areas (21.2\%), despite the classification already applying relaxed thresholds for rural mixed-traffic links (Section~\ref {sec:methodology}, Table~\ref {tab:mixed_traffic}).}}{24}{figure.caption.21}%
